            "note": "2021 Charles E. Peterson Prize, Third Place", 
            "label": ""
        }, 
        {
            "note": "The Samara House is a dwelling designed by Frank Lloyd Wright in the late  1950s. It is an excellent example of a &#8220;Usonian&#8221; style of the house designed by the world-renowned architect. Wright designed the architecture, landscape on the property, and was responsible for the fixed as well as movable furniture. The house stands testament today to Wright&#8217;s Usonian ideals and can be considered a magnificent example of his later work. It has been designated a National Historic Landmark structure in 2015. It is also listed in the Indiana Register of Historic Sites and Structures as of April 22, 1992, and in the National Register of Historic Places on December 16, 1992. ", 
            "label": "Significance"
        }, 
